<main class="wrap">
 <a class="back" href="/projects">← All projects</a>
 <div class="meta m"><span style="color:var(--ink)">Build 03</span><span class="ln"></span><span>Relational database engine</span><span class="ln"></span><span><span class="rec" style="animation:none;background:var(--ink)"></span>v1.0 · CI passing</span></div>

 <section class="top">
  <div>
   <h1 style="font-size:clamp(76px,12vw,184px)">ForgeDB</h1>
   <div class="kick">Everyone uses databases. <span>I built one to see what happens inside.</span></div>
   <p class="lede">A relational database engine in Java with no third-party libraries: its own SQL parser, binary records in 4 KB disk pages, an LRU buffer pool, persistent metadata and a B+ tree index. Every row is stored, found, updated and deleted by code I wrote.</p>
   <div class="acts"><a class="pillb solid" href="#query">Follow a query <span class="arr"></span></a><a class="pillb" href="#try">Try the shell</a><a class="pillb" href="https://github.com/VishalkiranRaichur/ForgeDB">Source</a></div>
  </div>
  <div class="facts rv">
   <div><span>Role</span><span>Solo · Java rebuild + tests</span></div>
   <div><span>Language</span><span>Java 17</span></div>
   <div><span>Dependencies</span><span>Zero</span></div>
   <div><span>Tested</span><span>20,000-key tree · restart persistence</span></div>
   <div><span>Runs as</span><span>Interactive SQL shell</span></div>
  </div>
 </section>

 <!-- 01 QUERY JOURNEY -->
 <section class="sec" id="query">
  <div class="sl m"><span><span class="n">01</span> · Follow a query</span><span>Pick one</span></div>
  <div class="sh"><h2 class="rv">One line of SQL. Seven layers of engine.</h2><p class="rv d1">Most projects talk to a database through a driver. ForgeDB goes one layer deeper. <b>Pick a query to see which parts of the engine it touches</b>, and why some queries are fast.</p></div>
  <div class="qj rv">
   <div class="qpick">
    <div class="m">Queries</div>
    <button class="qb on" data-q="0">SELECT * FROM students WHERE id = 2;<small>INDEXED LOOKUP</small></button>
    <button class="qb" data-q="1">SELECT * FROM students WHERE gpa &gt;= 3.5;<small>FULL SCAN</small></button>
    <button class="qb" data-q="2">INSERT INTO students VALUES (4, 3.6, 'Dev');<small>WRITE</small></button>
    <p class="qexp" id="qexp"></p>
   </div>
   <div class="stack" id="stack">
    <div class="lyr" data-l="shell"><span class="n">01</span><div><h5>Shell</h5><p>Interactive prompt, EXEC file.sql</p></div><span class="t"></span></div>
    <div class="lyr" data-l="parser"><span class="n">02</span><div><h5>SQL parser</h5><p>Text → immutable statement objects</p></div><span class="t"></span></div>
    <div class="lyr" data-l="engine"><span class="n">03</span><div><h5>Engine</h5><p>Routes each statement</p></div><span class="t"></span></div>
    <div class="split"><div class="lyr" data-l="catalog"><span class="n">04</span><div><h5>Catalog</h5><p>Schemas, keys, indexes</p></div><span class="t"></span></div><div class="lyr" data-l="record"><span class="n">05</span><div><h5>Record manager</h5><p>Rows ↔ binary</p></div><span class="t"></span></div></div>
    <div class="split"><div class="lyr" data-l="index"><span class="n">06</span><div><h5>B+ tree index</h5><p>Key → page + slot</p></div><span class="t"></span></div><div class="lyr" data-l="buffer"><span class="n">07</span><div><h5>Buffer pool</h5><p>LRU pages in memory</p></div><span class="t"></span></div></div>
    <div class="lyr" data-l="disk"><span class="n">08</span><div><h5>4 KB pages on disk</h5><p>students.records · students_pk.index</p></div><span class="t"></span></div>
   </div>
  </div>
 </section>

 <!-- 02 PAGE -->
 <section class="sec">
  <div class="sl m"><span><span class="n">02</span> · Inside a page</span><span>Exactly 4,096 bytes</span></div>
  <div class="sh"><h2 class="rv">Every table is a chain of 4 KB pages.</h2><p class="rv d1">Rows are encoded as fixed-length binary records. Each page starts with a <b>12-byte header</b> that links it to its neighbors and counts its rows. When deletes empty a page, ForgeDB <b>reuses it</b> instead of growing the file.</p></div>
  <div class="pg rv">
   <div class="m" style="margin-bottom:14px">One page</div>
   <div class="bytes"><div class="h"><small>BYTES 0–3</small><b>Prev page</b></div><div class="h"><small>BYTES 4–7</small><b>Next page</b></div><div class="h"><small>BYTES 8–11</small><b>Row count</b></div><div><div class="rows"><span>row 1</span><span>row 2</span><span>row 3</span><span>…</span></div></div></div>
   <div class="ruler"><span>0</span><span>12</span><span>FIXED-LENGTH ROWS (INT · FLOAT · CHAR(N))</span><span>4095</span></div>
   <div class="chain"><div class="pbox"><small>PAGE 0</small><b>prev −1</b></div><span class="lnk"></span><div class="pbox"><small>PAGE 1</small><b>full of rows</b></div><span class="lnk"></span><div class="pbox e"><small>PAGE 2</small><b>empty → reused</b></div><span class="lnk"></span><div class="pbox"><small>PAGE 3</small><b>next −1</b></div></div>
  </div>
 </section>

 <!-- 03 BTREE -->
 <section class="sec">
  <div class="sl m"><span><span class="n">03</span> · The B+ tree</span><span>Try a lookup</span></div>
  <div class="sh"><h2 class="rv">Find one row without reading every page.</h2><p class="rv d1">Each table gets a B+ tree on its primary key, with sorted, linked leaves and real leaf and internal splits as it grows. <b>An equality lookup walks a few nodes instead of scanning the whole table.</b></p></div>
  <div class="bt rv">
   <div class="g">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 560 260" id="tree">
     <g id="edges"></g><g id="nodes"></g>
    </svg>
   </div>
   <div class="s">
    <div class="m">Look up a key</div>
    <div class="findrow"><button data-k="7">id = 7</button><button data-k="33">id = 33</button><button data-k="58">id = 58</button><button data-k="91">id = 91</button><button data-k="40">id = 40</button></div>
    <div class="out" id="out">Pick a key to watch the search path.</div>
    <div style="margin-top:auto;padding-top:18px;border-top:1px solid var(--line);font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.6;color:#3a3936"><b style="font-weight:500">Design trade-off:</b> after deletes or primary-key changes, ForgeDB rebuilds the index. Simpler to reason about, while insert and search stay real B+ tree algorithms.</div>
   </div>
  </div>
 </section>

 <!-- 04 BUFFER POOL -->
 <section class="sec">
  <div class="sl m"><span><span class="n">04</span> · The buffer pool</span><span>Least recently used</span></div>
  <div class="sh"><h2 class="rv">Disk is slow. So keep the hot pages close.</h2><p class="rv d1">Recently used pages stay in memory. When the pool is full, the <b>least recently used</b> page is evicted, and if it was changed it's written back to disk first. Click pages to read or write them.</p></div>
  <div class="bp rv">
   <div><div class="m">Buffer pool · 4 slots</div><div class="slots" id="slots"></div>
    <div class="tally"><div><b id="hits">0</b><small>HITS</small></div><div><b id="miss">0</b><small>DISK READS</small></div><div><b id="fl">0</b><small>DIRTY FLUSHES</small></div></div></div>
   <div><div class="m">Request a page</div>
    <div class="reqs"><button data-p="1">read 1</button><button data-p="2">read 2</button><button data-p="3">read 3</button><button data-p="4">read 4</button><button data-p="5">read 5</button><button data-p="2" data-w="1">write 2</button><button data-p="6" data-w="1">write 6</button><button id="bpr" style="border-style:dashed">reset</button></div>
    <div class="blog" id="blog">Pool is empty. Every first read goes to disk.</div></div>
  </div>
 </section>

 <!-- 05 TESTS -->
 <section class="sec">
  <div class="sl m"><span><span class="n">05</span> · Proof</span><span>Tests + CI</span></div>
  <div class="sh"><h2 class="rv">Tested where databases usually break.</h2><p class="rv d1">Tree splits, page boundaries, deletes and restarts are where storage bugs hide, so that's where the tests push. <b>They run on every push with GitHub Actions.</b></p></div>
  <div class="tests">
   <div class="tc rv"><b>20<em>K</em></b><h5>Deep B+ tree</h5><p>20,000 keys with a tiny tree order to force internal splits and root growth.</p></div>
   <div class="tc rv d1"><b>1,000</b><h5>Storage stress</h5><p>Rows across many pages: deletes, page reuse, a primary-key update and an index rebuild.</p></div>
   <div class="tc rv d2"><b>↻</b><h5>Restart persistence</h5><p>Close the engine, reopen it, and verify every row and index survived.</p></div>
   <div class="tc rv"><b>SQL</b><h5>End-to-end smoke</h5><p>Create, insert, select, update and delete through the real parser and shell.</p></div>
  </div>
 </section>

 <!-- 06 TRY -->
 <section class="sec" id="try">
  <div class="sl m"><span><span class="n">06</span> · Try it</span><span>A real session</span></div>
  <div class="sh"><h2 class="rv">It's a real shell. Here's a session.</h2><p class="rv d1">Build it with one script, no Maven required, and run <b>java -jar build/forgedb.jar</b>. This replays the demo from the repo.</p></div>
  <div class="term rv"><div class="tb"><span>FORGEDB 1.0.0</span><span id="replay" style="cursor:pointer;color:#ff8a5c">↻ REPLAY</span></div><pre id="tty"></pre></div>
 </section>

 <!-- 07 SCOPE -->
 <section class="sec">
  <div class="sl m"><span><span class="n">07</span> · Scope</span><span>What's in, what's next</span></div>
  <div class="sh"><h2 class="rv">Small on purpose. Honest about it.</h2><p class="rv d1">ForgeDB covers the core path from SQL to bytes on disk. The features real databases add on top are the natural next steps, and <b>the hardest ones are the ones I'd build next</b>.</p></div>
  <div class="board rv">
   <div><h4>Built <span>08</span></h4>
    <div class="it">SQL subset: CREATE, INSERT, SELECT, UPDATE, DELETE<span class="s b">LIVE</span></div>
    <div class="it">WHERE with = &lt;&gt; &lt; &gt; &lt;= &gt;= and AND<span class="s b">LIVE</span></div>
    <div class="it">INT, FLOAT, CHAR(N) · primary-key checks<span class="s b">LIVE</span></div>
    <div class="it">Binary records in linked 4 KB pages<span class="s b">LIVE</span></div>
    <div class="it">Empty-page reuse after deletes<span class="s b">LIVE</span></div>
    <div class="it">LRU buffer pool with dirty flushing<span class="s b">LIVE</span></div>
    <div class="it">B+ tree primary-key index, indexed lookups<span class="s b">LIVE</span></div>
    <div class="it">Persistent catalog, records and index files<span class="s b">LIVE</span></div></div>
   <div><h4>Next <span>06</span></h4>
    <div class="it">Write-ahead log &amp; crash recovery<span class="s p">NEXT</span></div>
    <div class="it">Transactions &amp; rollback<span class="s p">PLANNED</span></div>
    <div class="it">Concurrent clients &amp; locking<span class="s p">PLANNED</span></div>
    <div class="it">Joins and column projection<span class="s p">PLANNED</span></div>
    <div class="it">Query planning &amp; cost optimization<span class="s p">PLANNED</span></div>
    <div class="it">Variable-length records<span class="s p">PLANNED</span></div></div>
  </div>
  <p class="claim rv">Not another app on top of a database. <span>The database itself, page by page.</span></p>
  <div class="chips rv"><span>Java 17</span><span>SQL parser</span><span>B+ tree</span><span>LRU buffer pool</span><span>Binary storage</span><span>GitHub Actions</span><span>Zero dependencies</span></div>
 </section>

